00:41:12.940 --> 00:41:15.040
The families hadn't known that before.

00:41:15.260 --> 00:41:17.820
They didn't know that the safety agency

00:41:17.820 --> 00:41:20.340
gambled with passenger lives.

00:41:20.740 --> 00:41:23.480
We knew that eventual solution

00:41:23.480 --> 00:41:25.400
would be to have the modification,

00:41:26.030 --> 00:41:27.850
and based on our risk assessment,

00:41:27.850 --> 00:41:29.570
we felt that this would be sufficient.

00:41:30.000 --> 00:41:36.160
time to be able to do the modification, you know, and get the final fix.

00:41:37.300 --> 00:41:44.100
After the Lion Air crash, the FAA had conducted an analysis of the likelihood of another 737

00:41:44.100 --> 00:41:45.140
Max crashing.

00:41:45.320 --> 00:41:48.830
The worst case scenario was grim.

00:41:49.900 --> 00:41:59.280
They looked at the probability that there could be another crash of a 737 Max if the

00:41:59.280 --> 00:41:59.960
FAA...

00:42:00.000 --> 00:42:03.640
didn't do anything to MCAS and just let the plane keep flying.

00:42:04.420 --> 00:42:11.840
And what that assessment showed was that FAA predicted there could potentially be 15 more

00:42:11.840 --> 00:42:22.640
fatal accidents of 737 MAX aircraft over the lifespan of the fleet, about one crash every

00:42:22.640 --> 00:42:23.320
other year.

00:42:24.700 --> 00:42:29.870
But in explaining its decision not to ground the plane, the FAA said in its statement,

00:42:30.000 --> 00:42:36.190
that the actual risk at the time, considering the number of planes in the air, was as close to zero

00:42:36.190 --> 00:42:44.400
as their calculations allowed. The agency had given Boeing 150 days to fix MCAS and issued

00:42:44.400 --> 00:42:50.880
official directives to pilots. They were gambling. They were betting against time that they would

00:42:50.880 --> 00:42:58.240
have a fixed MCAS before the next crash happened. And unfortunately, they lost that bet.

00:43:00.700 --> 00:43:04.340
Not everyone within the FAA agreed with the agency's gamble.

00:43:05.540 --> 00:43:10.340
People too quickly jumped to that conclusion that the pilot should have been able to figure out

00:43:10.340 --> 00:43:13.060
what's going wrong and be able to intervene properly.

00:43:15.150 --> 00:43:19.630
FAA engineer Joe Jacobson examined the data from the Lion Air crash

00:43:19.630 --> 00:43:22.950
and quickly raised concerns about the safety of the MAX.

00:43:23.930 --> 00:43:25.950
This is his first on-camera interview.

00:43:26.310 --> 00:43:28.130
I was pointing out a design flaw.

00:43:28.630 --> 00:43:29.870
It's purposely designed.

00:43:30.000 --> 00:43:34.600
and certified to use only one AOA input to drive MCAS

00:43:34.600 --> 00:43:36.940
to move the horizontal stabilizer at a high rate.

00:43:37.780 --> 00:43:40.380
Talked to three managers, said this is a design flaw.

00:43:40.940 --> 00:43:44.100
They were skeptical, not really buying in,

00:43:44.240 --> 00:43:46.360
saying the pilots should have been able to intervene.

00:43:47.240 --> 00:43:48.020
It's a failure.

00:43:50.050 --> 00:43:52.210
Our job is aviation safety.

00:43:52.210 --> 00:43:54.110
And when airplanes go down,

00:43:54.310 --> 00:43:59.610
we feel a real personal sense of loss and remorse.

00:44:00.540 --> 00:44:03.200
failure, and it affects a lot of people.

00:44:07.700 --> 00:44:12.090
In the fall of 2019, with the MAX having been grounded for seven months,

